WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:11.620
530 million miles from the sun, a tiny lump
of ice and rock starts its journey towards

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.000
the inner solar system.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.690
Scientifically, comets are more precious than
pure gold.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.180
They are actual time capsules.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.930
They are preservations of what the
chemistry, the environment was like when our

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.280
solar system formed.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.660
And they hold clues as to how our chemistry
arrived here on Earth.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.020
Comets are some of the most valuable
scientific treasures that exist.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.390
Finding a comet we can study is not an easy
task.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.360
Finding the right comet for a mission.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.520
It's kind of like you're auditioning them,
right?

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.800
One comes in and it's like, Oh, that orbit's
okay, but it's not exactly what we're looking

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.580
for. And oh, this one's too big, this one's
too little, this one's too active.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.750
What we really want to see is that comet come
from outer parts of the solar system and get

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.040
heated up as it gets close to the sun, pass
around the sun and head back out to the outer

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.120
part of the solar system. So you need to get
way out in the solar system and catch a comet

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.320
on its way in.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.130
Comet £0.67 fits the bill.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.930
The special thing about Comet £0.67 is how
accessible it is here on Earth.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.130
It actually orbits around the sun once every
six and a half years, and its orbit doesn't

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.400
take it all that far out, only about as far
out as the planet Jupiter.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.450
That gives us many chances to actually reach
the comet and successfully rendezvous with

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:30.810
it.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:32.590
Holy mackerel.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.420
67 PS moment in the spotlight arrived.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.830
The European Space Agency launched the
groundbreaking Rosetta probe.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.710
For years we've studied comets from afar.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:54.190
We had never seen one up close with cutting
edge technology to learn about how comets

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.290
behave as they orbit the sun.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.600
Missions to large planet sized objects are
hard.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:10.560
The Hubble Space telescope's blurry images
revealed Rosetta's target is just two miles

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.700
wide. And not just a small target, a moving
one.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:22.860
Comet £0.67 races through the solar system
at over 33,000 miles an hour.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.460
The precision involved is pretty incredible.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.180
It's like making that hole in one golf shot
from New York to San Francisco.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:36.520
But catching Comet £0.67 wasn't just a
straight shot.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.290
The spacecraft had to actually get the same
sort of velocity as the comet.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.910
And with current propulsion systems, we
can't achieve that by flying directly to the

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:46.420
comet.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:54.710
Instead, Rosetta performed a slingshot
maneuver that would take ten years.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.560
That's what it's doing. It's actually taking
some energy away from the planet.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.450
So it's slowing the planets down a little
bit and then imparting that energy into the

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:06.990
probe.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:17.040
Rosetta flew past Jupiter nearly 100 million
miles from the warmth of the sun and entered

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.470
the most dangerous part of its journey.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.040
Hibernation.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.480
Even though Rosetta had solar panels on it,
it still had to be put to sleep.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.460
And the reason for that is that it had to
track 60 7PA ways away from the sun and there

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.570
just was not enough collection area and
solar power to be able to power the

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:36.380
instruments.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.540
There's no way to put spacecraft into
hibernation.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.160
But the worrying thing at the back of your
mind is that it had never been done for this

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.030
long before.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:51.140
31 months of hibernation gave Rosetta's team
plenty of time to worry about what could go

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:51.830
wrong.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.010
It's dark and cold out there in space.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.510
There's a lot of things going on.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.190
There's a lot of little micrometeorites out
there.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:04.580
And without the constant communication with
that spacecraft, just as a little nerve

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.000
wracking when the day comes and it's time to
flip the switch and turn it back.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:17.660
On January 20th, 2014, after almost ten years
in space, it was finally time for Rosetta to

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.710
wake up, reactivate its communication system
and phone home.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.780
I mean, the tension was just palpable.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:31.200
It was just I mean, how long do we have to
wait before we're going to get the signal?

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.570
It's got to work. It has to work.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.760
Oh gosh, maybe it's just not going to turn on
