00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.710
Have we lost the spacecraft?

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.220
And then the peak appeared on the graph.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.390
It's like, yes, we've got contact with the
spacecraft.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.610
Rosetta began to send back images of its
target and after months of seeing a small.in

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:09.700
the distance, the comet slowly came into
focus.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:19.100
When I saw this comet crystal clear, this
mountain floating in space of ice and rock,

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:20.870
my heart just dropped.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.500
There are some of the most dramatic,
beautiful images I have ever seen.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.920
And then as it got closer and we got to see
more and more details on it, yeah, that's

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.470
when things started getting really strange.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.750
Quite simply, arrival at £0.67.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.740
We expected to see something shaped like a
potato and we found something shaped like a

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:43.580
rubber duck.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:51.620
£0.67 is no ugly duckling, but its strange
shape created a problem for Rosetta.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:58.040
Orbiting the comet was going to be far more
difficult than anyone had imagined.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:01.740
A planet has a lot of gravity.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.460
So you can send the spacecraft out there and
then just slow it down a little bit with a

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.680
rocket burn and it'll drop into orbit.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.360
In the case of comet £0.67, you're dealing
